      "PAN AM MANAGEMENT CLUB\nGALA DINNER\nHonoring San Francisco Management\nEmployees\nRetiring September 1, 1980\nAirport Sheraton Hotel\n7 p.m.\nAugust 26, 1980\nCLASS OF 1980\nNAME\nSam Acuff\nPeggy Brown\nKen Chan\nHarry Chew\nRay Choklat\nWyatt Fisher\nGene Garrison\nJohn Glass\nJane Gottschall\nLarry Hines\nSpike Horiye\nCal Howell\nMary Isiac\nGeorge Jean\nBob Johnson\nHerb Johnson\nPhil Jones\nYEARS OF\nSERVICE\n32\n14\nNAME\nAl Jordan\nCal Kennard\nSam Kinoshita\nEarl Koepsell\nTiger Lillie\nShorty Lovell\nDave Maloney\nHoward Marcks\nBill Marsh\nTex Marshall\nJoe Maschewski\nLou Mazzucchelli\nDon McMorran\nRin Navarro\nHerb Nelson\nCec Newton\nJoe Newton\nYEARS OF\nSERVICE\nLO\nYEARS OF\nSERVICE\n33\n33\n33\nNAME\nMilt Nordstrom\nJohn Oakes\nHarlan Pick\nCliff Pierce\nFrank Quigley\nNelson Raymond\nJim Robinson\nBill Rook\nFrank Scott\nEddie Shiosaka\nTerry Shores\nVern Smith\nFreeman Tong\nJohn Wendt\nGene Whitlock\nDennis Woodrich\nChet Zinkel\n33\n39\n34",
      "→»»»\u003e\u003e\u003e•»\u003e￾\n•1. 1.1•1.16.16.116\nMENU\nTossed Green Garden Salad\nChef's Dressing\nRoast Prime Rib of Beef au jus-Royal Cut\nRoasted New Potatoes with Butter and Paprika\nStringbeans with Almonds\nRolls\nButter\nDessert\nCoffee\nTea\nAlmaden Cabernet Sauvignon\nAlmaden Pinot Chardonnay",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"FLYING CLOUD\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print.\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Museum of the City of New York.\nLaunched in 1851, the \"Flying Cloud\" was the largest merchant ship afloat. In her brief career\nthe \"Flying Cloud\" was perhaps the greatest and the tallest of the China clippers. She set a half-\ndozen of the world's permanent sailing records, and reaped huge profits, once making nearly $200,000\non a single voyage. On her maiden run to California, the \"Flying Cloud\" sped to the Golden Gate\nin 89 days. Thereafter eighty-nine was the magic number for every other clipper captain.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world ... to Japan ... to Australia... and, in the days of the great\ngold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California. Our current\nequipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century ... consists of the finest commercial\naircraft ever developed —aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record for safety,\nspeed and service.\n*CUPPER TRADE MARK-REG US PAT OFFICE-COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS IN\nUHO IN USA -879-039\n2000. 05%0564"
